mode, entering FM, dragging playback to 93%, exiting, and verifying the loop mode description persists before restoring it to sequential playback.\n\nI'm also considering the harder case of testing the url=null failure cap — since I can't reliably reproduce this against the real server, I'm thinking about running a small local mock server reachable from the emulator via 10.0.2.2 that returns a personal_fm song with a null URL, though this would require network sandbox escalation and I'm weighing whether it's worth the effort.\n\nTracing the failure cascade: each failed URL triggers playFailed, increments a counter, and advances to the next track until it hits a cap and stops with a toast, keeping total requests bounded to roughly 4-5. I could also verify success paths with the mock — a 200 response for the trash endpoint, or multi-song playlists — which would at least simulate the logged-in flow even without real authentication, partially covering that open issue.\n\nBut the mock still needs to mimic the real API's response shapes for login status, song URL and detail, lyrics, and other endpoints the explore screen hits, since unmocked ones return 404 JSON that might break parsing if the app doesn't catch exceptions — worth testing to see if that causes a crash.\n\nFor playback, the best approach is proxying: let the mock server forward unoverridden routes to the real NeteaseCloudMusicApi at 127.0.0.1:3000, while overriding specific endpoints like personal_fm and fm_trash. For the multi-song scenario, I'd have personal_fm fetch three real songs by calling the real endpoint multiple times, verifying the queue shows three entries without triggering prefetch since we're not at the last index yet.\n\nI also want the mock server to log every request so tests can verify exact counts — one personal_fm call per rapid click burst, one fm_trash per dislike, mode switches recording the right parameter, and cancels producing no request at all. For the \"nourl\" sc","startTime":