f","type":"REASON","end":false,"content":"So each action is counted exactly once, meaning the duplicates I saw earlier came from the retry logic inside `request()` when the server returns a blacklisted or repeated song — since unauthenticated requests pull from a limited pool, and the blacklist grows as dislikes accumulate across tests, raising the odds of a retry. That explains the repeated hits in the explore tests. I want to double check whether this retry behavior is actually correct.\n\nThis matches acceptance criteria A6: if returned songs are all blacklisted/duplicated and filtering leaves nothing, the system should request once more. So a count of 2 for E1 in that edge case is expected, not a bug — any official test checking \"exactly one request per action\" would need to account for this. The real tradeoff is between replaying a blacklisted song or issuing a retry, and I think retry is the right choice to keep.\n\nTo make this testable, I should log the retry reason explicitly before firing it off, then have tests subtract retry-triggered requests from the total so they can assert \"1 per action, plus a logged retry when applicable.\" I'll add","startTime":1792311284707,"lastModifiedTime":1792311310381}]},{"id":"6ad48012e31d83197031d6eb","discard":true,"metrics":{},"hasUnpairedToolUse":false,"children":[{"id":"f0873c8b-84cf-4346-94bd-7daac0a00765","type":"REASON","end":false,"content":"This run shows no duplicates since each action maps to one request. The failures likely stem from the retry-on-filtered-empty path—blacklist/dedupe logic that's actually expected behavior per the spec, since repeated dislikes across test runs cause the blacklist to accumulate.\n\nI'm thinking I should add a log statement when retrying so tests can distinguish organic requests from retries—something like logging \"推荐结果已全部过滤，重新请求\" in the retry branch. That way tests can compute user-triggered requests as total minus retries, validating this is intentional design (A6/A12/A45 specify retrying once when filtered results are empty) rather than a bug to mask.\n\nI'm also worried about buffer rotation causing negative count deltas since other apps share the same log domain, so to make counting robust, I'll grab a device timestamp marker at test start and filter lines by parsing the `timestamp=` parameter from request URLs, counting only those at or after the marker—this makes rotation only risk undercounting (safe failure) rather than false positives for duplicate detection.\n\nNow I need a way to distinguish retry requests from fresh ones, since retries log the same event type but at a different time—I'll add an explicit timestamp to the retry log line itself so tests can compute successful requests minus retries within the same window, rather than relying on fragile before/after deltas.\n\nI'm computing the net count of e1 requests minus retries since a timestamp. The device clock only gives second prec","startTime":1792311320503,"lastModifiedTime":1792311356523}]},{"id":"6ad48043e2713b7e00edc09b","discard":false,"metrics":{"duration":16969},"hasUnpairedToolUse":false,"children":[{"id":"539ff09d-1348-4735-a293-28559d0b61cf","type":"REASON","end":true,"content":"Here the behavior looks correct: each dislike triggers one E2 and one E1 event, and exploring triggers one E1. The first log line requesting the personal FM station calls ensure_fm_playing, which only produces one E1 for entering. So in the failing run, the duplicate requests seem to be coming from the retry path—when a filter returns empty, it retries once, likely because the server returned a duplicate or blacklisted result.\n\nThis matches test_04's explore case: since multiple songs were disliked in earlier runs, the explore list may legitimately contain blacklisted entries, triggering the documented \"retry if filtered result is empty\" rule. That makes the counts nondeterministic, so I should add a distinct log marker for retries and have the test subtract retry counts from E1 