 loading yet. That means the second click is hitting the new song before it's actually playing, which violates the spec rule that a repeat click during the in-flight window before the new song starts should be ignored. The fix is to keep dislikeInFlight true until playback actually begins (via onPlayStart) rather than releasing it right after queuing.\n\nThinking about timing more carefully: clicks happen roughly every 0.2-0.4s, and three clicks could land within about a second, close to when the song URL actually updates — so there's some residual risk, but the fix is still semantically right since \"new song starting\" should align with when the name actually updates on screen after setPlayData/onPlayStarted.\n\nAdding a waiter mechanism in FMUtils...\n\nI'm reasoning through edge cases: if the next song fetch fails and we don't switch, the dangling waiter just resolves later via timeout harmlessly since nothing awaits it, and a subsequent dislike would simply overwrite playSettled safely. Also confirming that onPlayFailed's retry path still resolves the waiter correctly since it triggers another play attempt.\n\nI'm also checking whether referencing `done` inside the setTimeout callback before its const declaration causes issues in ArkTS — normally block-scoped closures can reference variables declared later since the callback runs after, but ArkTS might be stricter about this, so I'm restructuring the timer/done logic to avoid relying on that pattern, clearing the timer and nulling out playSettled only if it still points to this specific done callback before resolving.\n\nI'm also confirming the type signature for playSettled as an optional void-returning function, setting a 10-second timeout, and making sure notifyPlaySettled safely invokes and clears it. For rapid next-button clicks, each click should just trigger a normal skip since that's expected behavior, so no extra guarding is needed there before implementing everything.\n\n","startTime":1792311860889,"l